{
  "id": "phase-116",
  "title": "Fail an attempt fast when it has written no implementation",
  "severity": "major",
  "depends_on": [],
  "risk": "medium",
  "finding": "An attempt can burn its entire budget and produce nothing, and the pipeline discovers this only at the very end. phase-053 ran three times. Attempt 1 had a 90-minute budget and spent all of it reading \u2014 `grep -rn ... src/`, `ls src/repl/`, then reading whole files including phase-052's freshly created REPL modules \u2014 followed by `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `cargo clippy`, and was killed by the timeout mid-verification. It never created a test file and never wrote a line of implementation. The evidence it left behind is unambiguous: `work-phase-053.patch` is 0 bytes and `phase-053.checkpoint.log` is 0 bytes, so there was never any work to preserve or recover. AGENTS.md's method is 'reproduce, write the failing test FIRST, then change code', and nothing enforces step 2 or 3 \u2014 so an agent that is slow, or cautious, or reading a large codebase can spend the whole budget in step 1 and be killed with nothing to show. The 90-minute timeout is doing its job by bounding the damage; what is missing is a cheap check partway through that turns a dead 90-minute run into a 5-minute one.",
  "goal": "Add a progress watchdog to the dispatch step that detects an agent which has produced no implementation partway through its budget, and ends the attempt early instead of waiting for the timeout. The check must be evidence-based, not time-guessing: the agent's own workspace is inspected for a test file it created or a change under its `must_touch` areas, and the watchdog fires only when the budget is well past the point where that should have happened. Implement nothing in Redblue; this is entirely rbops dispatch logic, so `must_touch` is `rbops/`. Do not shorten the overall attempt budget \u2014 a legitimate hard phase that writes tests early must keep its full time. The goal is to kill attempts that have demonstrably produced nothing, not to make all attempts shorter. Declare this phase by hand: it changes rbops, which the bot token cannot push.",
  "accept": [
    "an attempt that has written no test file and touched no `must_touch` area past a stated fraction of its budget is ended early, and the log names the fraction, the elapsed time, and what was checked",
    "the watchdog fires on a fixture agent that only reads and verifies, and does NOT fire on one that writes a test file early even if it later takes the full budget",
    "the early end is recorded as an attempt that consumed nothing and produced no `.deferred`, `.failed` or `.blocked` marker \u2014 so it cannot masquerade as a deferral",
    "the full budget is still granted to an attempt that writes its test file within the first fraction of the window, verified by a fixture that sleeps past the watchdog but writes first",
    "the watchdog's own absence is loud: if the check cannot determine whether work happened, it says so and lets the attempt continue rather than failing a phase on a guess",
    "the attempt budget is unchanged for phases that do implement early \u2014 asserted against the dispatch timeout constant, not a comment",
    "a unit test in the rbops suite covers each of the two directions: 'reads only, gets killed' and 'writes first, survives'",
    "REPORT.md records the measured saving on the fixture, and states plainly that this reduces wasted time rather than adding capability"
  ],
  "manual": true,
  "timeout_min": 120
}
